{"title":"2014 AIME I Problems/Problem 3","text":"<div class=\"mw-content-ltr mw-parser-output\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><div id=\"toc\" class=\"toc\" role=\"navigation\" aria-labelledby=\"mw-toc-heading\"><input type=\"checkbox\" role=\"button\" id=\"toctogglecheckbox\" class=\"toctogglecheckbox\" style=\"display:none\" /><div class=\"toctitle\" lang=\"en\" dir=\"ltr\"><h2 id=\"mw-toc-heading\">Contents</h2><span class=\"toctogglespan\"><label class=\"toctogglelabel\" for=\"toctogglecheckbox\"></label></span></div>\n<ul>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-1\"><a href=\"#Problem_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">1</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Problem 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-2\"><a href=\"#Solution_1_(Euclidean_algorithm,_fast)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">2</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 1 (Euclidean algorithm, fast)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-3\"><a href=\"#Solution_2\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">3</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 2</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-4\"><a href=\"#Solution_3\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">4</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 3</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-5\"><a href=\"#Solution_4\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">5</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 4</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-6\"><a href=\"#Solution_5\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">6</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 5</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-7\"><a href=\"#Solution_6\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">7</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 6</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-8\"><a href=\"#Solution_7\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">8</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 7</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-9\"><a href=\"#Solution_8_(Simplest)\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">9</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 8 (Simplest)</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-10\"><a href=\"#Solution_9\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">10</span> <span class=\"toctext\">Solution 9</span></a></li>\n<li class=\"toclevel-1 tocsection-11\"><a href=\"#See_also\"><span class=\"tocnumber\">11</span> <span class=\"toctext\">See also</span></a></li>\n</ul>\n</div>\n\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Problem_3\">Problem 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=1\" title=\"Edit section: Problem 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Find the number of rational numbers <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/0/4/804f706e4f51e30c6a8bc1fd4100cfa7d6abf5cf.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$0&lt;r&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"77\" height=\"15\" />, such that when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/5/5/b55ca7a0aa88ab7d58f4fc035317fdac39b17861.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> is written as a fraction in lowest terms, the numerator and the denominator have a sum of 1000.\n</p><p><br />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_1_.28Euclidean_algorithm.2C_fast.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_1_(Euclidean_algorithm,_fast)\">Solution 1 (Euclidean algorithm, fast)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=2\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 1 (Euclidean algorithm, fast)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Let the numerator and denominator <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/6/4/b6400c6fe3f1ed70e5afd387a9686cdc3fa09531.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x,y$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"27\" height=\"11\" /> with <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/4/3/8435f42a4f0210c104760ca8bf732716c92f8bdb.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x,y)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"105\" height=\"20\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/5/7/157f6f2658772daa1db7c6e582b46bedfcbd26b2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x+y = 1000.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"106\" height=\"16\" /> Now if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/f/08faa9e8ddd8bcadc02a1d8749837647097b7a05.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x,y) = 1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"105\" height=\"20\" /> then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/8/a/f8a460481e29e8bb60c8e3870f11ab671453b71b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x,y) =\\gcd(x,1000-x)= \\gcd(x,1000-x-(-1)x)=\\gcd(x,1000)=1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"606\" height=\"20\" /> Therefore any pair that works satisfies <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/3/a336f7f5bdf8fe9ede9fb48b0e94674370f4fdbc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(x,1000)= 1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"136\" height=\"20\" /> By Euler's totient theorem, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/2/e/e2e4d0870a9d3628af9216d5b882f3345d00e365.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\phi(1000) = 400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"112\" height=\"18\" /> numbers relatively prime to 1000 from 1 to 1000. Recall that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/7/4/47410c5f14aeccaea3ea90b5d6f4070ba7dded68.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=\\frac{x}{y}&lt;1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -17px\" width=\"82\" height=\"38\" /> and note by Euclidean algorithm <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/f/4/af4d960363ca6cd0e062dc0eb0a9de9b1442e872.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(1000,1000-x)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"190\" height=\"20\" />, so we want <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/5/e/25e455904df5fb506ebd97498996b11e3d80cbd3.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x&lt;y=1000-x.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -4px\" width=\"144\" height=\"17\" /> Thus the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e8924412a12becf7905729885a46e3e351ec5a86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> relatively prime numbers can generate <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/4/d54c01bf8b58951d7f6775fa650b26155f55102c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{200}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" /> desired fractions.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_2\">Solution 2</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=3\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 2\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>If the initial manipulation is not obvious, consider the Euclidean Algorithm. Instead of using <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/a/2/da291d8501e98fabcab1e8b34d109251f06e6203.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{n}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"18\" height=\"33\" /> as the fraction to use the Euclidean Algorithm on, we can rewrite this as <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/8/c/c8c9f240570087fc295cfa6af11bd5bf62ec6f38.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{500-x}{500+x}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -14px\" width=\"62\" height=\"39\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/1/9/1/19194dfc2ab5528680092bd1bf8b61bd58f1c8f5.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[\\gcd(500+x,500-x)=\\gcd((500+x)+(500-x),500-x)=\\gcd(1000,500-x).\\]\" width=\"589\" height=\"18\" /> Thus, we want <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/8/1/a81094102ce62b28a589a2b1f30b507aa05a11c5.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\gcd(1000,500-x)=1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"181\" height=\"20\" />. You can either proceed as Solution <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, or consider that no even numbers work, limiting us to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/8/448b3c8f2596d1b93b4b3ef199f8b3241ced9da2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$250$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> choices of numbers and restricting <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> to be odd. If <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/6/e/26eeb5258ca5099acf8fe96b2a1049c48c89a5e6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$x$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"12\" height=\"10\" /> is odd, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/a/86a9d5344091f5ade65c0a4728185f5d9284c4ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$500-x$\" width=\"59\" height=\"12\" /> is odd, so the only possible common factors <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee927e1332358c96c62c277441c907c4f51057f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/6/a/86a9d5344091f5ade65c0a4728185f5d9284c4ad.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$500-x$\" width=\"59\" height=\"12\" /> can share are multiples of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, we want to avoid these. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906734e2f1010e9a171ffee8050cbc0649cea37c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$50$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> odd multiples of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> less than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/9/a39b14023c98c943554d150561aacc188c4ce26d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$500$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/d/a1d4003394b5086761ad3ee387c2752b299ea53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$250-50=\\boxed{200}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"132\" height=\"25\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_3\">Solution 3</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=4\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 3\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Say <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/4/9/e49e008ca03f9bccf04383807e757426fccd85f0.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$r=\\frac{d}{1000-d}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"103\" height=\"38\" />; then <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/d/d/6dd461c7ea29bdd0b4fab7237b87a76dddbf06a9.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1\\leq d\\leq499$\" style=\"vertical-align: -3px\" width=\"96\" height=\"17\" />. If this fraction is reducible, then the modulus of some number for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/6/a/96ab646de7704969b91c76a214126b45f2b07b25.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> is the same as the modulus for <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/0/0/60047f460c61ebebe63dd6b3317a1d438992c906.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000-d$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"68\" height=\"13\" />. Since <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/c/2/7c226d89716e776032ed3f4b750929443519ca2e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000=2^3\\cdot5^3$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"109\" height=\"17\" />, that modulus can only be <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. This implies that if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/f/d7fc11cbb28785dca182a85c843879dbd4dc287f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d\\mid2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"36\" height=\"20\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/8/298ad6ff88311c5eb38a5b914026d98278ed404d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d\\mid5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"36\" height=\"20\" />, the fraction is reducible. There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/7/8f70a88401805d41dd703dc15c65ff8880289e86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$249$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> cases where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/7/f/d7fc11cbb28785dca182a85c843879dbd4dc287f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d\\mid2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"36\" height=\"20\" />, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/1/a313bf06349648df7be807bee95b651604ce7a7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$99$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/2/9/8/298ad6ff88311c5eb38a5b914026d98278ed404d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d\\mid5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"36\" height=\"20\" />, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/c/34c8e6bf4d57cd6f2bb0d7d43e00f0f442f53b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$49$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> where <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/c/8/9c8bd39ee198efa1dddbfcd104b53992722a05b6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$d\\mid(2\\cdot5=10)$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"114\" height=\"20\" />, so by PIE, the number of fails is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/e/1/fe1badfbd11d08c1e53f2d27fca96dae4b664105.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$299$\" width=\"26\" height=\"12\" />, so our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/4/d54c01bf8b58951d7f6775fa650b26155f55102c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{200}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_4\">Solution 4</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=5\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 4\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We know that the numerator of the fraction cannot be even, because the denominator would also be even. We also know that the numerator cannot be a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> because the denominator would also be a multiple of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Proceed by listing out all the other possible fractions and we realize that the numerator and denominator are always relatively prime. We have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/0/6500248a8e3334fcfb20b237b0e343980327ad17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$499$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> fractions to start with, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/8/448b3c8f2596d1b93b4b3ef199f8b3241ced9da2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$250$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> with odd numerators. Subtract <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906734e2f1010e9a171ffee8050cbc0649cea37c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$50$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> to account for the multiples of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, and we get <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/4/d54c01bf8b58951d7f6775fa650b26155f55102c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{200}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_5\">Solution 5</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=6\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 5\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We know that the set of these rational numbers is from <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/b/0/fb092b6ee16439070cbdab5db78fa1b2670420ab.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1}{999}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"29\" height=\"37\" /> to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/9/9/09951ae78a60da06d4012ec58d9d9e1ed4abf5bc.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{499}{501}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"29\" height=\"38\" /> where each each element <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/5/59522000a92a54c1695d2a56797eab215c4144ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{n}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"18\" height=\"33\" /> has <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/e/f/7ef01023d7f1d2c26195f0e1212a57ffdbe2e761.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$n+m =1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"109\" height=\"14\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/5/59522000a92a54c1695d2a56797eab215c4144ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{n}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"18\" height=\"33\" /> is irreducible. \n</p><p>We note that <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/7/3/b735c90021ea050f8d605cf1cd7c8eb35b041868.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{n}{m} =\\dfrac{1000-m}{m}=\\dfrac{1000}{m}-1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"217\" height=\"37\" />.\nHence, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/9/5/59522000a92a54c1695d2a56797eab215c4144ef.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{n}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"18\" height=\"33\" /> is irreducible if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/c/34cd58c13f0c28e73a7b3a7c69f715a73f5954c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1000}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"38\" height=\"37\" /> is irreducible, and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/c/34cd58c13f0c28e73a7b3a7c69f715a73f5954c4.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\dfrac{1000}{m}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -12px\" width=\"38\" height=\"37\" /> is irreducible if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/5/0/f5047d1e0cbb50ec208923a22cd517c55100fa7b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$m$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"18\" height=\"10\" /> is not divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />. Thus, the answer to the question is the number of integers between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/4/bd4cf10c283207e2482b3bc70a47489a0ba7fce6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$501$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/0/0705411d92671faf1fe5602cfef8f353c7f0f83d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$999$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> inclusive that are not divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />.\n</p><p>We note there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/0/6500248a8e3334fcfb20b237b0e343980327ad17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$499$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> numbers between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/4/bd4cf10c283207e2482b3bc70a47489a0ba7fce6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$501$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/0/0705411d92671faf1fe5602cfef8f353c7f0f83d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$999$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" />, and\n</p>\n<ul><li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/7/8f70a88401805d41dd703dc15c65ff8880289e86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$249$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> numbers are divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/3/1/a313bf06349648df7be807bee95b651604ce7a7e.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$99$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> numbers are divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /></li>\n<li><img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/4/c/34c8e6bf4d57cd6f2bb0d7d43e00f0f442f53b84.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$49$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"17\" height=\"13\" /> numbers are divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/f/c/6/fc606f7f1e530731ab4f1cc364c01dc64a4455ee.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$10$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /></li></ul>\n<p>Using the Principle of Inclusion and Exclusion, we get that there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/5/6/7/5670ccb471e5c4f6cce1ff476b310b2df2f72313.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$499-249-99+49=200$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"208\" height=\"14\" /> numbers between <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/b/d/4/bd4cf10c283207e2482b3bc70a47489a0ba7fce6.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$501$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/7/0/0705411d92671faf1fe5602cfef8f353c7f0f83d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$999$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> are not divisible by either <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/1/c/41c544263a265ff15498ee45f7392c5f86c6d151.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$2$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so our answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/4/d54c01bf8b58951d7f6775fa650b26155f55102c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{200}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />.\n</p><p>Euler's Totient Function can also be used to arrive at <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e8924412a12becf7905729885a46e3e351ec5a86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> numbers relatively prime to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee927e1332358c96c62c277441c907c4f51057f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />, meaning <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/3/8f37626d49b4082c9102bf930bca51277ff2eb43.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$200$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> possible fractions satisfying the necessary conditions. Solution 1 is a more detailed solution utilizing Euler's totient.\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_6\">Solution 6</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=7\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 6\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We notice that there are a total of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/e/8/9/e8924412a12becf7905729885a46e3e351ec5a86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> fractions that are in simplest form where the numerator and denominator add up to <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/e/e/6ee927e1332358c96c62c277441c907c4f51057f.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"38\" height=\"14\" />. Because the numerator and denominator have to be relatively prime, there are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/7/a/97ab64b40ef241acdde0c2c35999ac6951098b2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\varphi(1000)=400$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"116\" height=\"20\" /> fractions. Half of these are greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, so the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/3/5/7/35744dea1f4c0049eebaa351d83be9dc33479bdd.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$400\\div2=\\boxed{200}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"123\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p>- bedwarsnoob\n</p><p>~MathFun1000 (Minor Edits)\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_7\">Solution 7</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=8\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 7\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Our fraction can be written in the form <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/f/f/9ff8cef39bd8f4a9ad15fefd69f438cf92595f27.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{1000 - a}{a} = \\frac{1000}{a} - 1.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"175\" height=\"38\" /> Thus the fraction is reducible when <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> divides <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/b/4/6b434ebc2071e706df740975a3c90aad3cfa1455.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1000.$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"39\" height=\"13\" /> We also want <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/5/5/055931a9b87d166b487584993991be44d4dc9860.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$500 &lt; a &lt; 1000.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"129\" height=\"15\" /> By <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=PIE\" class=\"mw-redirect\" title=\"PIE\">PIE</a>, the total values of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> that make the fraction reducible is, \n<img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/4/8/0489b609f4053542a9dd139f8d743231f18eba1e.png\" class=\"latexcenter\" alt=\"\\[249 + 99 - 49 = 299.\\]\" width=\"162\" height=\"13\" />\nBy <a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=Complementary_counting\" title=\"Complementary counting\">complementary counting</a>, the answer we want is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/e/6/0e6f471ddf9a1b6a7652400f0d52a2eb0f4ef52d.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$499 - 299 = \\boxed{200}.$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"146\" height=\"25\" />\n</p>\n<h2><span id=\"Solution_8_.28Simplest.29\"></span><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_8_(Simplest)\">Solution 8 (Simplest)</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=9\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 8 (Simplest)\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>Suppose our fraction is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/0/8/4/084079bf1d54500f3c59257a7ec3467932b72ba2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\frac{a}{b}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -13px\" width=\"15\" height=\"34\" />. The given condition means <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/c/1/cc17074e83ec9d7299fe6a2c752ab5da6a720a2b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b=1000$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"99\" height=\"14\" />. Now, if <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/c/7/d/c7d457e388298246adb06c587bccd419ea67f7e8.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"10\" /> and <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/1/3/8136a7ef6a03334a7246df9097e5bcc31ba33fd2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"10\" height=\"14\" /> share a common factor greater than <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/c/e/dce34f4dfb2406144304ad0d6106c5382ddd1446.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$1$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, then the expression <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/d/d/add2609af1bb7632cc22b68fd49fdd94de06b327.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$a+b$\" style=\"vertical-align: -2px\" width=\"41\" height=\"16\" /> must also contain that common factor. This means our fraction cannot have a factor of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> or be even.\n</p><p>There are <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/8/448b3c8f2596d1b93b4b3ef199f8b3241ced9da2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$250$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> fractions that aren’t even. From this, <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906734e2f1010e9a171ffee8050cbc0649cea37c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$50$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> are divisible by <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" />, which means the answer is <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/a/1/d/a1d4003394b5086761ad3ee387c2752b299ea53b.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$250-50=\\boxed{200}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -6px\" width=\"132\" height=\"25\" />\n</p><p>~Geometry28-\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"Solution_9\">Solution 9</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=10\" title=\"Edit section: Solution 9\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<p>We can see that by picking a numerator for the fraction, the denominator will be forced. For the condition of the problem to be true, the numerator and denominator must be relatively prime. We know that the numerator can not be even, eliminating <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/8/f/7/8f70a88401805d41dd703dc15c65ff8880289e86.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$249$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> fractions out of the total <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/6/5/0/6500248a8e3334fcfb20b237b0e343980327ad17.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$499$\" style=\"vertical-align: 0px\" width=\"26\" height=\"13\" /> possible. We now have <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/4/4/8/448b3c8f2596d1b93b4b3ef199f8b3241ced9da2.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$250$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"29\" height=\"14\" /> fractions left. We notice that we must eliminate all multiples of 5 in the numerator, as these fractions will simplify. We subtract the <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/9/0/6/906734e2f1010e9a171ffee8050cbc0649cea37c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$50$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"20\" height=\"14\" /> multiples of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/7/9/0/79069377f91364c2f87a64e5f9f562a091c8a6c1.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$5$\" style=\"vertical-align: -1px\" width=\"11\" height=\"14\" /> to get our answer of <img src=\"//latex.artofproblemsolving.com/d/5/4/d54c01bf8b58951d7f6775fa650b26155f55102c.png\" class=\"latex\" alt=\"$\\boxed{200}$\" style=\"vertical-align: -5px\" width=\"39\" height=\"23\" />.\n~jamespudi\n</p>\n<h2><span class=\"mw-headline\" id=\"See_also\">See also</span><span class=\"mw-editsection\"><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">[</span><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_3&amp;action=edit&amp;section=11\" title=\"Edit section: See also\">edit</a><span class=\"mw-editsection-bracket\">]</span></span></h2>\n<table class=\"wikitable\" style=\"margin:0.5em auto; font-size:95%; border:1px solid black; width:80%;\">\n<tbody><tr>\n<td style=\"background:#ccf;text-align:center;\" colspan=\"3\"><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I\" title=\"2014 AIME I\">2014 AIME I</a></b> (<b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems\" title=\"2014 AIME I Problems\">Problems</a></b> • <b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Answer_Key\" title=\"2014 AIME I Answer Key\">Answer Key</a></b> • <a rel=\"nofollow\" class=\"external text\" href=\"http://www.artofproblemsolving.com/Forum/resources.php?c=182&amp;cid=45&amp;year=2014\">Resources</a>)\n</td></tr>\n<tr>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rowspan=\"1\">Preceded&#160;by<br /><b><a href=\"/wiki/index.php?title=2014_AIME_I_Problems/Problem_2\" title=\"2014 AIME I Problems/Problem 2\">Problem 2</a></b>\n</td>\n<td width=\"50%\" align=\"center\" rows